{
  "content": {
    "arguments": "$ARGUMENTS",
    "constraints": [
      {
        "id": "3f64a5f6-4ef0-495c-9e5d-c65a97060dd0",
        "text": "Use the Bash tool. The Pebbles CLI at \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" is the ONLY sanctioned way to change spec files — never edit .pebble files directly."
      },
      {
        "id": "c52e8f6e-930e-4101-93eb-a7c6be275a9c",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Extraction — workflow graph standard (rules/system/workflow-extraction)"
      },
      {
        "id": "21d3ee21-f5a1-4bbb-8e8a-ff5a410ac082",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Spec mutation contract (rules/system/spec-mutation-contract)"
      },
      {
        "id": "d75e3a03-3d71-4f44-b013-bdfbe1fb19e4",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Extraction — completeness standard (rules/system/extraction-standard)"
      },
      {
        "id": "4ddb5533-d3e1-4bef-9a79-f3f849273e2a",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Extraction — scope boundary (topic is law) (rules/system/extraction-scope)"
      },
      {
        "id": "429b8626-a167-4bde-9d99-d755b2330f3d",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Table completeness (rules/system/table-completeness)"
      },
      {
        "id": "521d9a8b-3ab0-4570-8ac7-dce7fef55b96",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Reference integrity (rules/system/reference-integrity)"
      },
      {
        "id": "d6e77595-5aba-4b1d-bdb9-4be6a73c2906",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Status discipline (rules/system/status-discipline)"
      },
      {
        "id": "b6558629-c9b6-4a36-9506-7391e03a61e7",
        "text": "Bound by system rule: Graph-first navigation (rules/system/graph-first-navigation)"
      }
    ],
    "examples": [],
    "kind": "skill",
    "onFailure": [
      {
        "id": "a2381c3a-b865-4903-ba7f-b702c4472ee9",
        "text": "Read the CLI error — it names the fix; correct the call and retry once"
      },
      {
        "id": "b62a579a-d98e-400f-86cc-1318a47a254e",
        "text": "NEVER fall back to editing .pebble files directly, whatever failed"
      },
      {
        "id": "59b6a9a7-c144-4e70-81b1-1f48c9ca5b92",
        "text": "If still blocked, report the exact command and error to the user and stop"
      }
    ],
    "output": "A parent workflow with subprocess links; child workflows created OR linked to existing ones (reconciliation report: created/linked/excluded with reasons); audit-pass evidence.",
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "id": "edb841df-07bd-4c72-80d1-45adc5e7fd73",
        "text": "A CURRENT project map exists (--fresh if stale)"
      },
      {
        "id": "db62664a-200e-4153-8e9f-59242e1a8e48",
        "text": "The entry point resolves to ONE concrete file:symbol — candidates confirmed with the user first"
      }
    ],
    "purpose": "Multi-level extraction: the topic + its callees as linked child workflows",
    "steps": [
      {
        "id": "7dfe8bb0-3762-439d-9440-c00fde1d1fe7",
        "text": "Deep extraction — multi-LEVEL, centered on ONE topic: $ARGUMENTS"
      },
      {
        "id": "ae698b9a-a1ae-4389-b3c3-f6decaa65da2",
        "text": "KIND GUARD: this extracts a WORKFLOW (flow graph) and its child workflows only. If a non-workflow pebble is focused (model/structure/api/checklist), STOP and tell the user to use /pebbles-extract instead — shallow/deep are workflow-only. With no focus, you are creating a new WORKFLOW pebble."
      },
      {
        "id": "1577dfd1-94fc-4800-90de-a4dbc6ad1f80",
        "text": "SCOPE BLOCK FIRST (echo before reading code): entry point(s) as file:symbol (ASK if ambiguous), depth: deep, the exclusion list. The closure is FORWARD-ONLY: code reachable from the entry point via calls/imports. Upstream callers and siblings are OUT — never extract the processes that lead HERE. REQUIRED: a current map (--fresh if stale)."
      },
      {
        "id": "b8757fc0-21c5-4814-b5c7-5833963b5fa4",
        "text": "RECONCILE BEFORE CREATE — for every non-utility callee, in order: (1) \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" map-file <its file> → the pebbles[] already bound to it; (2) query by symbol name; (3) a candidate with the SAME entry identity (file:symbol) → LINK it as the subprocess, stop; (4) names differ but the candidate's cited locations cover ≥70% of the callee's closure → LINK it and note the alias; otherwise CREATE and ref the near-match as related. A symbol already extracted THIS RUN always links the existing pebble — cycles never re-extract."
      },
      {
        "id": "c6347c65-02d0-42d6-8ebf-fa16a357384b",
        "text": "ENVELOPE PATTERN: a controller that opens a transaction, delegates the core mutation to a domain service, then maps result→response — the domain call becomes a subprocess linking the child; the API layer stays high (transaction, auth gate, post-commit events, error taxonomy live in the parent; domain detail in the child). PROMOTION TEST (subprocess vs plain step, per non-utility callee, first match wins): (0) utilities — formatters/mappers/validators/DTOs/thin wrappers → KEEP as step, never promote; (1) count its DISTINCT operations (branches, I/O, dispatched events, domain steps — not lines): <6 → KEEP as a step (a callee that would graph to ~2–5 nodes is too small for its own workflow — note its ops in the description); ≥6 → continue; (2) NAMEABLE as a noun-phrase and self-contained? no → KEEP; yes → (3) PROMOTE: own child workflow, ONE subprocess node in the parent, recurse the test in the child. Examples — WorkOrderService::create() (validate+persist+side-effects+event, ~8 ops) = PROMOTE; WorkOrder::create() direct model insert = KEEP; dispatch(OrderPlaced) with 6 real listeners = PROMOTE; dispatch(UserLoggedIn) writing a timestamp = KEEP; an imported file whose flow is only 3 nodes = KEEP. The bar is per-callee; it NEVER caps the parent (a 40-step parent stays 40 nodes). FORBIDDEN: promoting every import (subprocess explosion) OR collapsing a real ≥6-op service into one vague step. PARENT AND EVERY CHILD independently terminate every path (one terminal per distinct outcome; success/error ARE end nodes). ERROR HANDLING BY SCOPE: a single-purpose guard stays inline (check → own error → end); a region of MANY actions under a handler → an exception check → its OWN `exception` node → its OWN end in front of that region (DUPLICATED like success/error — several per flow, never reuse or point back to one), with the handler modeled once as a `global-exception` flow in the SAME pebble (unconnected nodes off to the right, not a separate pebble/subprocess) — NEVER loop an error arrow back up to a reused box. TRY/CATCH (EVERY language — YOU identify the guarded error-handling construct yourself: try/catch, try/except, begin/rescue, do/catch, defer+recover, …, not by keyword) → the dedicated `try`/`catch` nodes (CLI-enforced): `try` `{` = TWO outputs (BODY that returns to the catch + SPINE straight to the catch); the BODY is modeled in full (inner guards keep their own condition + a throw → `error` \"<what> — error thrown\" → end); `catch` `}` = TWO inputs (spine + body return) and TWO outputs — the EXCEPTION line labeled \"Exceptions\" (fixed) → the failure handling and the CONTINUATION line with NO label → the code after the try/catch. CAUGHT vs UNCAUGHT: a catch that HANDLES locally → real steps → end (no global-exception); a catch that RE-THROWS/delegates, or an UNCAUGHT throw with no enclosing try/catch → an `exception` hand-off → end → a `global-exception` flow (exception ⟹ global-exception). Never invent a global-exception for a self-contained catch. `arrange` draws the bracket (first body node aligned horizontally with the try, rest flowing down). Shared utilities (fan-in ≥5 codebase-wide: log/validate/auth/serialize/config) are NEVER nodes. CAPTURE EVERY BRANCH AND EXIT (any language) — every conditional/switch-case/loop-exit/throw/return and every catch/except/rescue CLAUSE (multi-clause = several handlers, each modeled distinctly, never merged); before finishing, list the code's decision + exit points and confirm each maps to the graph. Max recursion depth 3."
      },
      {
        "id": "8de4bf10-5b61-4527-b45e-819d56845ffa",
        "text": "CO-LOCATE CHILDREN with the parent: create each child workflow in the SAME folder as the parent workflow so the user finds it right beside it. If the parent lives in a subfolder (e.g. .pebbles/workflows/orders/parent.pebble), pass \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" create workflows --dir orders --title \"...\"; if the parent is at the section root, omit --dir. Never scatter children into a different folder."
      },
      {
        "id": "c54f0ed9-5285-4565-90f7-f58d5d381dc9",
        "text": "Each extracted/linked child becomes a subprocess node in the parent (the CLI enforces the link). Every node at every level cites a code location inside the closure."
      },
      {
        "id": "1a529b6c-8886-4bc4-825a-669558d6726e",
        "text": "ARRANGE each workflow you touched: \"{PEBBLES_CLI}\" arrange <path> (never hand-place nodes). AUDIT PASS before declaring done: re-derive the node list from the citations alone and diff it against what you drew; then sample 3 in-closure branches (each must appear) and 3 out-of-closure neighbors (each must NOT). Report: created N child workflows, linked M existing, excluded K (with reasons)."
      }
    ],
    "verify": [
      {
        "id": "f73a756c-4314-4f71-8686-eff72919701a",
        "text": "The Scope Block (entry point, forward closure, exclusions) was echoed before any write"
      },
      {
        "id": "d6d1825f-d625-40bd-ac6e-870c405eac31",
        "text": "Existing pebbles were reconciled BEFORE creating — report lists created vs linked vs excluded"
      },
      {
        "id": "31285e1e-8ed8-439e-a585-8510dd57f040",
        "text": "Every node at every level cites a location inside the closure; no upstream/sibling content"
      },
      {
        "id": "faed6342-1756-4977-bc9b-5e78ce9ce724",
        "text": "The audit pass ran: citation re-derivation diff + 3-in/3-out negative sampling"
      }
    ],
    "whenToUse": "ONE topic in full detail PLUS promoting its qualifying callees (services/events/imported files with REAL internal flow — ≥6 distinct ops and nameable) into their OWN child workflows, subprocess-linked and reconciled before creating. A one-op import (model.create()) or a tiny 2–5 node flow stays a described step. Can also run top-level, heaviest of the three."
  },
